class:: PitchContour
summary:: Causal dynamic-programming tracker for candidate arrays
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Pitch
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/Pitch2, Classes/FundamentalCandidates, Classes/PitchProb

description::
Uses a fixed-capacity candidate graph and Viterbi-style min-sum dynamic programming: negative log observation weight plus continuity, octave and voicing transition costs. This follows the general candidate-tracking approach used by probabilistic pitch estimators such as Mauch and Dixon (ICASSP 2014); the sparse state graph and transition formula are MIRa conventions, not the pYIN HMM. A ring of 32 predecessor rows supports bounded traceback. There is no hidden pitch analyzer. Frequency zero marks the unvoiced state; confidence is the selected observation weight, not a posterior path probability.

Like link::Classes/Pitch::, this family extracts control signals from sound; it exposes explicit evidence or contour information for synthesis control.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: candidateFreqs
Array of 1 through 16 candidate frequencies in Hz. Zero means absent.

argument:: candidateProbabilities
Matching Array of nonnegative weights. Sum below 1 reserves unvoiced mass; sums above 1 are normalized.

argument:: maxJump
Cents per update before an additional transition cost of 8 is applied. This is a soft limit.

argument:: transitionWeight
Nonnegative cost multiplier for absolute cents motion divided by maxJump.

argument:: octavePenalty
Additional nonnegative cost for octave-related transitions within 80 cents.

argument:: unvoicedPenalty
Nonnegative cost for switching between voiced and unvoiced states.

argument:: trig
Positive values advance the candidate graph once this control block. Supply a hop-rate impulse to process each analysis observation once; default 1 advances every block.

argument:: lag
Literal traceback delay from 0 through 31 update ticks. Zero returns the latest best-path endpoint.

returns::
An Array: tracked frequency in Hz, selected candidate observation probability, signed pitch jump in cents.

examples::
PitchContour performs Viterbi-style dynamic programming over a multi-candidate pitch graph with octave and voicing penalty costs and bounded traceback lag. In this example, it stabilizes pitch candidates extracted from an expressive saxophone solo, eliminating octave skips and tracking smooth glissandi with a vintage theremin sine voice. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.pianoRoll:: plots the tracked Viterbi contour.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("melody.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic melody fallback
            Saw.ar(Select.kr(LFNoise0.kr(2).range(0, 5), [220, 261.63, 293.66, 329.63, 392, 440])) * 0.2
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var candidates = FundamentalCandidates.kr(source, maxCandidates: 4, minFreq: 80, maxFreq: 1200, frameSize: 2048, hop: 0.25);
        var tick = Impulse.kr(SampleRate.ir / 512);
        var track = PitchContour.kr(candidates[0], candidates[1], trig: tick, lag: 2);
        var freq = track[0].clip(50, 2000);
        var theremin = SinOsc.ar(freq.lag(0.02), 0, track[1].lag(0.04) * 0.16 * (track[0] > 50));
        var sound = (source * 0.25) + theremin;

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, track[0]) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.pianoRoll("Viterbi Pitch Contour", ~miraBus, 36, 84);
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
